src/harness/channel.hpp-1-// The control channel: a loopback TCP listener that never touches the engine.
src/harness/channel.hpp-2-//
src/harness/channel.hpp-3-// One thread does socket / bind / listen / accept and then select() over the
src/harness/channel.hpp:4:// listen socket and the one client. It splits what arrives into lines and
src/harness/channel.hpp-5-// queues them; it writes what the main thread queued back. It never parses a
src/harness/channel.hpp-6-// request, never reads engine memory, never sees a lua_State. Parsing happens
src/harness/channel.hpp-7-// on the main thread under the fault guard, and execution happens there too,
src/harness/channel.hpp-8-// because that is the only thread on which engine calls are legal.
src/harness/channel.hpp-9-//
src/harness/channel.hpp:10:// One client at a time. A second connection gets one JSON error line and is
src/harness/channel.hpp-11-// closed, so "who is driving" is never ambiguous. Every request carries the
src/harness/channel.hpp:12:// client generation it arrived on; a reply for a client that has since gone
src/harness/channel.hpp-13-// is dropped rather than delivered to whoever connected next.
src/harness/channel.hpp-14-//
src/harness/channel.hpp-15-// Loopback only. A bind address outside 127.0.0.0/8 is refused at start(),
src/harness/channel.hpp-16-// not narrowed: the harness runs unauthenticated Lua in the game's process.
src/harness/channel.hpp-17-//
src/harness/channel.hpp-18-// Speak the protocol or leave. Loopback is not a trust boundary against a
src/harness/channel.hpp:19:// browser: a page that POSTs here sends a request line and headers, then a
src/harness/channel.hpp:20:// body line it chose, possibly all in one segment. So the first line that is
src/harness/channel.hpp:21:// not a JSON object condemns its connection (condemn()): the drain runs
src/harness/channel.hpp:22:// nothing more from that generation, stale or queued, and the listener sends
src/harness/channel.hpp:23:// the one error reply and closes. A reconnect is a new generation and has to
src/harness/channel.hpp:24:// be well-formed from its first line.
src/harness/channel.hpp-25-//
src/harness/channel.hpp-26-// Wine's ws2_32 maps AF_INET onto host sockets, so this same file serves the
src/harness/channel.hpp-27-// DLL under Wine and the native Linux build; only the includes differ.
src/harness/channel.hpp-28-#pragma once
