     1	// The control channel: a loopback TCP listener that never touches the engine.
     2	//
     3	// One thread does socket / bind / listen / accept and then select() over the
     4	// listen socket and the one client. It splits what arrives into lines and
     5	// queues them; it writes what the main thread queued back. It never parses a
     6	// request, never reads engine memory, never sees a lua_State. Parsing happens
     7	// on the main thread under the fault guard, and execution happens there too,
     8	// because that is the only thread on which engine calls are legal.
     9	//
    10	// One client at a time. A second connection gets one JSON error line and is
    11	// closed, so "who is driving" is never ambiguous. Every request carries the
    12	// client generation it arrived on; a reply for a client that has since gone
    13	// is dropped rather than delivered to whoever connected next.
    14	//
    15	// Loopback only. A bind address outside 127.0.0.0/8 is refused at start(),
    16	// not narrowed: the harness runs unauthenticated Lua in the game's process.
    17	//
    18	// Speak the protocol or leave. Loopback is not a trust boundary against a
    19	// browser: a page that POSTs here sends a request line and headers, then a
    20	// body line it chose, possibly all in one segment. So the first line that is
    21	// not a JSON object condemns its connection (condemn()): the drain runs
    22	// nothing more from that generation, stale or queued, and the listener sends
    23	// the one error reply and closes. A reconnect is a new generation and has to
    24	// be well-formed from its first line.
